<template><div class="site"><a class="skip-link" href="#guide">Skip to guide</a><SiteHeader/><main id="guide" class="guide content-width"><header class="guide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Field guide</p><h1>How to play RAID OPS</h1><p class="lead">Learn the controls, choose an operative, and use the right tools for each fight. This guide covers the desktop browser game at runandgun.games.</p></header><article class="prose" data-guide-content>
<h2>Start or continue a game</h2><p>Choose Start game on the homepage to open campaign selection. If you already have a compatible saved game, a dialog first offers Continue saved game or New game. Continue restores the saved battlefield and opens Pause, so you can get ready before selecting Resume. New game opens campaign selection; the existing save is replaced only when you deploy into the new attempt. Earned weapons, shared research and best scores are kept.</p><p>Quick start takes you directly into the first Grayhaven mission with your selected operative. It is useful for trying the controls without going through deployment. When there is no existing save, Quick start also saves your attempt automatically. If you already have a saved battlefield, a quick attempt leaves it untouched, including when you pause, restart or leave. You can still earn permanent weapons and research. Use Start game and New game to replace an existing continuing attempt deliberately.</p><p>Regular attempts automatically save every few seconds and when you pause, leave the mission or close the page normally. The save includes your position, health, weapon, enemies, supplies, vehicles, machinery and objective progress. An abrupt browser or device crash can lose the last few seconds. Only one continuing attempt is stored in this browser. Grayhaven has six missions; Sandstorm has one desert mission. Completing a mission removes its continuing save, and deployment into the next mission creates a new one.</p>
<h2>Keyboard controls</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A / D</td><td>Move left or right</td></tr><tr><td>Space / K</td><td>Jump</td></tr><tr><td>J</td><td>Hold to fire</td></tr><tr><td>W / S</td><td>Aim upward / crouch; climb while on a ladder</td></tr><tr><td>Q / 1–6</td><td>Cycle unlocked weapons / select an unlocked weapon</td></tr><tr><td>U</td><td>Use your operative's skill</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>Interact with a rope, ladder, vehicle or battlefield facility</td></tr><tr><td>L</td><td>Throw a grenade</td></tr><tr><td>R / Esc</td><td>Retry or restart / pause or resume</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Click inside the battlefield if keyboard focus has moved elsewhere. Holding J fires at your weapon's rate rather than producing a single shot. Aim assist helps direct fire toward suitable visible targets, but bullets still travel through the world. A solid beam or wall can stop the shot. Change your position, jump for a better angle, or use the route above the obstruction instead of firing into the same cover.</p>
<h2 id="operatives">Choose Falcon or Silver Fox</h2><div class="gameplay-grid"><article><h3>Falcon: Tactical dash</h3><p>Falcon is selected by default. The deployment bar shows your operative and skill; use Change operative to switch to Silver Fox. Your choice is remembered in this browser for future attempts, so you can deploy without choosing again. Falcon's tactical dash uses U to move quickly in the direction you face and gives brief damage protection. It is useful for crossing a firing lane, but it does not pass through walls or locked gates. Watch the skill indicator for the cooldown rather than assuming another dash is ready immediately.</p></article><article><h3>Silver Fox: Aerial flip</h3><p>Silver Fox trades the dash for an aerial flip. While airborne, press U or press jump again to gain an extra leap. The skill is limited to once per airtime and also has a cooldown. Use it to recover a short jump or reach a higher route. Your operative is fixed during a mission attempt, so make this choice at the deployment screen rather than expecting to switch characters in the middle of a fight.</p></article></div>
<h2 id="weapons">Switch weapons for the target</h2><p>The assault rifle is your starting weapon. Capture armory encounters to add the shotgun, piercing rifle, machine gun, rocket launcher and EM cannon to your available loadout. Unlocks are permanent progress. Press Q repeatedly to cycle through the weapons you actually own, or use a number key from 1 to 6 for a direct choice. Locked weapons cannot be selected.</p><div class="gameplay-grid">
<article><h3>1 / Assault rifle</h3><p>Your starting weapon delivers steady fire while you learn enemy positions. Hold J to keep shooting and move between clear firing lanes. If a beam blocks the shot, change height or position.</p></article>
<article><h3>2 / Shotgun</h3><p>A spread of pellets works best at close range. Approach clustered infantry from a safe angle, fire, then move before the next volley. Across a long firing lane, switch to another weapon.</p></article>
<article><h3>3 / Piercing rifle</h3><p>Piercing rounds can damage multiple targets along their path and work well against armored enemies. Line up the formation before firing. Solid terrain can still stop your shot.</p></article>
<article><h3>4 / Machine gun</h3><p>A high firing rate helps maintain pressure and track drones. Watch targets above you as well as enemies on the ground, and leave your firing position before incoming shots surround it.</p></article>
<article><h3>5 / Rocket launcher</h3><p>Explosive rounds can affect grouped enemies and destructible cover. Find a clear angle toward an armored target or a crowded defensive position before firing into it.</p></article>
<article><h3>6 / EM cannon</h3><p>The EM cannon is useful against energy defenses. Select it when those defenses complicate the fight, and check the in-game weapon guide for its strengths against the current target.</p></article>
</div><p>Weapon research is shared across your arsenal. When research reaches another level, the upgrade notice explains the changes for your current weapon. A newly captured weapon benefits from the research already earned instead of starting with a separate training level. At mission results, check any new weapon reward and its selection key before beginning the next operation.</p>
<h2 id="equipment">Use platforms and battlefield equipment</h2><h3>Ladders, ropes and ziplines</h3><p>Not every obstacle needs to be destroyed. Climb a ladder with E and then use W or S to change height. At a swing rope, E attaches you to the rope and allows a crossing with momentum. Fixed ziplines use a different motion: attach near the trolley with E, use A or D to travel along the cable, and dismount automatically at either bank. Space can release you early, so judge the landing before jumping off over a gap.</p><h3>Fixed guns, cranes and vehicles</h3><p>At a fixed gun, press E to take control and J to fire. A and D change its facing. E or Space leaves the gun. Firing builds heat, and an overheated gun needs time to cool. Cargo cranes let you move a suspended load before dropping it; a supported cargo landing can create a useful platform. Clear the crew around an available armored vehicle before boarding. With aim assist enabled, its turret can track visible enemies independently of the direction you drive.</p><h3>Terminals and facilities</h3><p>Facilities differ by location. A control panel may operate a freight lift, gate or industrial hazard. An outpost terminal needs you to hold position during the hack, and taking damage interrupts the process. Destroying certain power or radar equipment can disable nearby defenses. Read the prompt at the actual facility; the interaction key is shared, but the result depends on what you are operating.</p>
<h2 id="bosses">Read bosses and avoid marked attacks</h2><h3>Grayhaven: Harbor tank</h3><p>Boss fights reward noticing the current stage. Grayhaven's harbor tank has separate tracks, side gun and engine components. Break the tracks first, then the side gun, and finish the exposed engine. The glowing target marker shows the current weak point. The tank can still retaliate during the engine stage, so use the gap after a firing sequence to attack rather than staying beside the hull indefinitely.</p><h3>Sandstorm: Sand Scorpion</h3><p>Sandstorm's Sand Scorpion has a different rhythm. Its wheels enable a charge, and its engine vent opens between cannon attacks after the wheels are broken. Leave a marked mortar landing area before the explosion. Ground warnings and sight lines tell you where an attack is preparing to land; watch the shape and location of the warning, then move, jump or crouch as appropriate. A boss description is a useful starting point, but the live warning is what tells you when to react.</p>
<h2>Retries, supplies and leaving a mission</h2><p>Health, armor and grenades are separate resources. Supplies restore the resource shown by their icon, while checkpoints provide a retry position during the current mission. A retry restores the battlefield from that checkpoint and keeps permanently earned weapons and research. The checkpoint snapshot is also kept inside a regular attempt’s saved game, so continuing later preserves its retry position. Check your surroundings after a retry before repeating the same jump or attack.</p><p>Press Esc or use Pause to stop play. The pause dialog provides an exit confirmation that returns to mission selection. Leaving a regular attempt saves the battlefield for the Start game dialog; a Quick start attempt does not replace the continuing save. Sound and background music can be adjusted in settings. The game also offers reduced motion. If you switch away from the tab, background audio is silenced rather than continuing the firefight behind another page.</p><p>For a fresh attempt, open the game again and choose the campaign you want to play. If you use a different device, browser or domain, that environment has a separate local save. Clearing runandgun.games site data removes the records and saved battlefield kept there. An incompatible or damaged save cannot be continued; your separate permanent unlocks and records remain available if their data is intact.</p>
<h2 id="settings">Audio and comfort settings</h2><p>Open Settings to adjust sound effects and music independently. The browser may wait for a click before allowing audio: use Enable background music or start playing to permit it. Switching away from the tab silences the game. Aim assist and automatic fire can also be changed in Settings. Reduced motion disables screen shake, flips and blinking if those effects make the action harder to follow.</p>
</article><div class="actions"><NuxtLink to="/" class="button">Play RAID OPS</NuxtLink><NuxtLink to="/" class="text-link">Back to home</NuxtLink></div></main><SiteFooter/></div></template>
